<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William VIII, Duke of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1025–<a href="/years/1086">1086</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Guy-Geoffrey · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William VIII, born Guy-Geoffrey, was Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Poitou from 1058 to 1086. A son of William V the Great, he secured the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> in the 1050s and 1060s, uniting it with Aquitaine and extending ducal lordship from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> expedition of 1064 and founded the abbey of Montierneuf at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-viii-duke-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="William VIII, Duke of Aquitaine" width="304" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Archeos at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TombeauG8poitiersface.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William VIII was born Guy-Geoffrey, the youngest son of William V of Aquitaine by his third wife Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His sister Agnes of Poitou married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, which made him the emperor's brother-in-law. He became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> during the rule of his older brother William VII. Gascony had come under Aquitanian rule through William V's marriage to Prisca of Gascony, sister of Duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony. He succeeded William VII as Duke of Aquitaine and Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and held those titles until his death on 25 September 1086.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>William VIII was one of the leaders of the allied army called to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-aragon">Ramiro I of Aragon</a> at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> in 1064. This was the first campaign organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, under Pope Alexander II, against a Muslim town and stronghold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and a precursor of the later crusading movement. Aragon and its allies conquered the city, killed and enslaved its inhabitants and collected important booty, though Aragon lost the city again in the following years.</p><p>During his rule an alliance with the southern kingdoms of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was a political priority, as shown by the marriage of all his daughters to Iberian kings. His daughter Agnes married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a>, and another Agnes married Peter I of Aragon.</p><p>He married three times and had at least five children. He divorced his first wife, Garsende of Périgord, because of infertility, and she became a nun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>. He divorced his second wife, Matoeda, in May 1068. His third wife was a much younger cousin, Hildegarde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, daughter of Duke Robert I of Burgundy. Their children included his heir William IX of Aquitaine. In the early 1070s he had to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to persuade the pope to recognise the children of this marriage as legitimate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William VIII of Aquitaine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Great, who ruled from the 990s until 1030, made the dukes among the most respected princes of the kingdom, and his son <b>Guy-Geoffrey</b>, who reigned as William VIII, acquired the Duchy of Gascony in the 1050s and 1060s, extending ducal lordship from the Loire to the Pyrenees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a succession struggle, <b>Guy-Geoffrey</b>, count of Poitou, secured Gascony in the 1050s and united it with the Duchy of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-gascony">Duchy of Gascony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Chizé, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1064" aria-label="Poitiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4558 526 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William VIII, Duke of Aquitaine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5008 796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5008 796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1064">What was here in 1064? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
